# Architecture and boundaries

Created: 2026-11-04T21:45:23Z (first recorded addition in Git)
Last updated: 2026-11-05T16:46:28Z

## Project direction

Spatial System develops geospatial systems using Rust, Python, Swift, and SQL,
with longer-term goals in edge and distributed computing.

The first major project objective is a Rust-based point-cloud query engine
built on Apache DataFusion. This is the main architectural direction today.
It is an objective, not a claim that the engine is already implemented.

Distinguish:

- Implemented capability, supported by inspected code and verification.
- Accepted architectural direction, recorded in project decisions.
- Proposed work that still requires evaluation and agreement.

A roadmap, syllabus, or architectural proposal does not authorize implementation.

## Architecture evolves

These rules are expected to change as the project develops.

When an agreed decision changes the architecture, update the affected rules
and architectural records as part of that scoped change. Explain what changed
and why; do not silently treat an earlier rule as obsolete.

Inspect relevant code, decisions, and current task context before proposing
changes. Surface discrepancies between them for resolution.

## Repository map

Current project and supporting locations:

- `rust/spatial-core/`: existing Rust package. Inspect its implementation before
  describing its capabilities or deciding where new engine components belong.
- `docs/research/`: investigations, findings, limitations, and retrospectives.
- `docs/decisions/`: architectural decisions and their rationale.
- `sprints/`: scoped project plans and progress.
- `rust/learn/`: separately governed coursework and supporting learning material.

Expected growth:

- Rust engine components organized around agreed responsibilities.
- Python components for agreed analysis, tooling, or integration needs.
- Swift components for agreed client or device capabilities.
- SQL interfaces and queries associated with the engine.

Decide concrete directories and package boundaries when the relevant work is
scoped. Record agreed future locations here as proposed until implemented.
Do not create placeholder packages or directories merely to represent the roadmap.

## Noncanonical and potentially legacy material

`benchmarks/` contains earlier experimental work created before this ruleset.
It is potentially legacy material and is not part of the canonical project
architecture.

Its structure, dependencies, environments, and workflows are not defaults for
new project work. Do not extend it merely because related tooling already exists.

Existing material may be inspected as historical evidence. Retaining, adopting,
relocating, replacing, or removing it requires a separately agreed task.
Noncanonical status does not authorize cleanup or invalidate recorded evidence.

## Coursework boundary

Coursework is colocated for convenience and follows its applicable AGENTS.md
and syllabus guidance.

Project work must not modify exercises, progress, or curriculum unless explicitly
included in the request. Coursework completion does not establish project capability.

Promotion of an exercise into reusable project code requires explicit agreement.
Do not move historical exercises as routine architectural cleanup.

## Component boundaries

Keep responsibilities clear and proportionate to the agreed task.

- Separate core computation from filesystem, network, process, and interface
  concerns where this improves understanding and verification.
- Expose explicit inputs, outputs, errors, and relevant data contracts.
- Keep executable entry points focused on composing operations.
- Avoid hidden initialization and implicit mutable state across components.
- Introduce abstractions for demonstrated needs rather than hypothetical reuse.

These principles do not require a new framework, package, configuration system,
or architectural layer for a small task.

Reusable project code must not depend on coursework or noncanonical experimental
runners. Adoption of experimental code requires agreement on its responsibility,
interface, dependencies, correctness requirements, and maintenance expectations.

## Point-cloud engine direction

Use Apache DataFusion as the intended foundation for SQL planning, expression
evaluation, and aggregation. Evaluate suitable existing readers for point-cloud
decoding and access rather than assuming custom implementations are necessary.

Consult the relevant architectural decisions for detailed engine contracts.
Identify any proposed departure and discuss it before implementation.

Preserve coordinate meaning across component boundaries, including CRS, axis
units, scale/offset, and the distinction between sampled visualization and
full-resolution analytical data.

The engine direction does not automatically authorize bindings, services,
clients, distributed execution, or supporting infrastructure.

## Language and integration boundaries

Introduce Rust, Python, Swift, and SQL components when an agreed task justifies
them. Language support in the roadmap does not require tooling installation now.

Before introducing a boundary between languages or processes, explain:

- Its purpose and the data crossing it.
- Data types, ownership or copying, and relevant coordinate metadata.
- Error behavior and resource lifecycle.
- How the boundary will be verified.

Apply the bounded-assignment and delegation rules in
[coauthoring_protocol.mdc](coauthoring_protocol.mdc).
